{"page": 34, "text": "The following Management\u2019s Discussion and Analysis of Financial Condition and Results of Operations (MD&A) should be read together with the unaudited consolidated financial statements and accompanying notes for the period ended September 30, 2026 included under \u201cItem 1. Financial Statements\u201d of this Form 10-Q and the audited consolidated financial statements and related notes and MD&A included in Item 8 and 7, respectively, of our Annual Report on Form 10-K for the year ended December 31, 2025. This MD&A includes forward-looking statements that involve certain risks and uncertainties. See \u201cForward-Looking Statements\u201d at the end of this section. Overview Murphy is an independent oil and natural gas company with a multi-basin onshore and offshore portfolio and significant exploration opportunities. The Company boasts over a century of strong execution and innovative, full-cycle development capabilities, with a focus on value creation to enhance shareholder returns. The Company\u2019s current operations include inventory located onshore in the Eagle Ford Shale, Tupper Montney and Kaybob Duvernay, as well as offshore in the Gulf of America, Canada and Vietnam. Murphy also strives to create long-term shareholder value through offshore exploration and development in the Gulf of America, Vietnam and C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire. The analysis and discussion in this section includes amounts attributable to the noncontrolling interest in MP GOM, unless otherwise noted. Significant Company financial and operational highlights during the third quarter of 2026 were as follows: \u2022Production was 192,283 barrels of oil equivalent (BOE) per day (including NCI), a decrease from 206,936 BOE per day in the third quarter of 2025; \u2022Advanced the Bubale appraisal program offshore C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire, with the Bubale West-1X well; \u2022Drilled an oil discovery at the Lac Da Trang (White Camel) North-1X exploration well offshore Vietnam; \u2022Achieved first oil from the Lac Da Vang (Golden Camel) development offshore Vietnam; \u2022Commenced production from the Chinook #8 development well in the Gulf of America; and \u2022Brought online 14 operated and four non-operated Eagle Ford Shale wells, and eight wells in the Tupper Montney. Subsequent to the third quarter: \u2022Secured six exploration blocks from the August Gulf of America federal offshore lease sale. \u2022Signed agreements with the government of Mauritania for two offshore exploration blocks Murphy Oil Corporation\u2019s net income from continuing operations, including noncontrolling interest, for the three months ended September 30, 2026 was $195.8 million compared to net loss of $7.8 million for the same period in 2025. The increase in 2026 was primarily driven by higher revenues from production ($128.2 million), no impairment charges in the current year ($115.0 million), and lower lease operating expenses ($35.2 million). These favorable items were partially offset by higher income tax expense ($35.2 million), and higher other operating expenses ($22.7 million).\n"}
{"page": 35, "text": "Higher revenues were driven by higher realized prices in the United States and Canada. These favorable impacts were partially offset by lower volumes in the Eagle Ford Shale and the Gulf of America. The prior-year period included impairment charges related to the Dalmatian field, while no impairment charges were recorded in the current year. Lower lease operating expenses were primarily driven by lower workover activity, insurance proceeds received related to past workovers, and production-related costs in the Gulf of America, partially offset by increased activity in the Eagle Ford Shale and Canada. Higher income tax expense primarily reflected higher pretax income. Higher other operating expenses primarily related to abandonment and environmental remediation costs in the Gulf of America and Canada, respectively. For the three months ended September 30, 2026 total hydrocarbon production was 192,283 barrels of oil equivalent per day, a decrease of 7% compared to the third quarter of 2025. The decrease was principally due to lower volumes in the Gulf of America and the Eagle Ford Shale. Lower Gulf of America volumes primarily 29 Table of Contents ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT\u2019S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS (CONTINUED) Overview (Continued) reflected natural declines at various wells, and lower Eagle Ford Shale and Tupper Montney volumes primarily reflected the timing of new wells. These decreases were partially offset by higher Canada Offshore volumes due to less downtime at Terra Nova. Net income from continuing operations, including noncontrolling interest, for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was $529.0 million, an increase of $412.9 million compared to the same period of 2025. Higher net income from continuing operations was primarily driven by higher revenues from production ($431.1 million), lower lease operating expenses ($168.6 million), no impairment charges in the current year ($115.0 million), and higher other income ($38.6 million). These favorable items were partially offset by higher income tax expense ($128.4 million), higher exploration expenses ($97.8 million), higher depreciation, depletion and amortization (DD&A) ($51.0 million), and higher other operating expenses ($34.4 million). Higher revenues were primarily driven by higher realized crude oil prices across the United States and Canada, together with higher sales volumes at Eagle Ford Shale, Kaybob and Terra Nova. These favorable impacts were partially offset by lower oil sales volumes in the Gulf of America and lower natural gas sales volumes at Tupper Montney. Lower lease operating expenses were primarily driven by substantially lower workover and production-related costs in the Gulf of America, partially offset by higher activity in the Eagle Ford Shale and higher repair, maintenance and workover costs in Canada. The prior-year period included impairment charges, while no impairment charges were recorded in the current year. Higher\n"}
{"page": 36, "text": "other income was primarily due to favorable foreign exchange movements. Higher income tax expense primarily reflected higher pretax income. Higher exploration expenses were primarily driven by dry hole costs in C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire and Vietnam. Higher DD&A was primarily driven by higher sales volumes and depletion rates in the Eagle Ford Shale, higher rates in the Gulf of America and higher sales volumes at Kaybob and offshore Canada, partially offset by lower sales volumes in the Gulf of America and at Tupper Montney. Higher other operating expenses primarily related to abandonment and environmental remediation costs in the Gulf of America and Canada. For the nine months ended September 30, 2026, total hydrocarbon production was 182,494 barrels of oil equivalent per day, a decrease of 3% compared to the same period in 2025. The decrease was principally due to lower volumes in the Gulf of America as a result of natural decline, and at Tupper Montney in Canada due to fewer new wells online in the current year. These decreases were partially offset by new Eagle Ford Shale production. Murphy\u2019s continuing operations generate revenues through the production and sale of crude oil, natural gas and natural gas liquids in the United States and Canada. Changes in the price of crude oil and natural gas have a significant impact on the profitability of the Company. In order to make a profit and generate cash in its exploration and production business, revenue generated from the sales of oil and natural gas produced must exceed the combined costs of producing these products and expenses related to exploration, administration and capital borrowing from lending institutions and note holders. International conflicts and geopolitical uncertainty surrounding domestic and foreign governmental regulations, including effects of trade policies, tariffs and other trade restrictions, can affect the demand for crude oil, natural gas and natural gas liquids, as 