.\nIncome Taxes Income taxes for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026 increased by $42.8 million and $118.3 million, respectively, compared to the same periods in 2025. Higher income tax expenses were primarily due to higher pretax income in the United States and Canada. The tax impact was also affected by exploration expenses incurred in foreign jurisdictions where no income tax benefits are currently available. The Company also recognized an income tax deduction for prior years\u2019 Australia exploration spend.\n"}
{"page": 44, "text": "Corporate Corporate activities include interest expense and income, foreign exchange effects, realized and unrealized gains/losses on derivative instruments (forward swaps to hedge commodity price) and corporate overhead not allocated to E&P. Realized and unrealized gains and losses on derivative instruments result from changes in market natural gas prices relating to future periods whereby the swap contracts provided the Company with a fixed price. The Corporate segment reported a loss of $31.5 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026, an unfavorable variance of $12.9 million compared to the same period in 2025. The unfavorable variance was primarily due to lower foreign exchange gains ($11.3 million) and the non-repeat of prior year derivative gains ($5.7 million), partially offset by a higher income tax benefit ($7.7 million). The Corporate segment reported a loss of $99.9 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, a favorable variance of $22.8 million compared to the same period in 2025. The favorable variance was primarily due to higher foreign exchange gains ($41.5 million) and was partially offset by higher net interest expense associated with the 2034 Notes and a lower income tax benefit ($10.1 million).\nFinancial Condition The Company\u2019s primary sources of liquidity are cash on hand, net cash provided by continuing operations activities and available borrowing capacity under its Amended RCF. The Company\u2019s liquidity requirements, both in the short-term (2026) and long-term (beyond 2026), consist primarily of capital expenditures, debt maturity, retirement and interest payments, working capital requirements, dividend payments, and, as applicable, share repurchases. The Company may, from time to time, redeem, repurchase or otherwise acquire its outstanding notes through open market purchases, tender offers or pursuant to the terms of such securities. The Company believes that the primary sources of liquidity described above will be adequate to fund its liquidity needs over the next 12 months and the foreseeable future.\n39 Table of Contents ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT\u2019S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS (CONTINUED) Financial Condition (Continued) Cash Flows The following table presents the Company\u2019s cash flows for the periods presented:\nNine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n(Millions of dollars) 2026 2025\nNet cash provided (required) by:\nNet cash provided by continuing operations activities $ 1,517.6 $ 998.2\nNet cash required by investing activities (1,361.7) (851.4)\nNet cash required by financing activities (32.8) (143.9)\nEffect of exchange rate changes on cash and cash equivalents \u2014 (0.5)\nNet increase in cash and cash equivalents $ 123.1 $ 2.4\n"}
{"page": 45, "text": "Cash Provided by Continuing Operations Activities Net cash provided by continuing operations activities for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 increased by $519.4 million compared to the same period in 2025. The increase in cash flows from operations activities was primarily due to higher realized commodity prices, which resulted in an increase in revenue from production ($431.1 million), and lower lease operating expenses ($168.6 million), primarily driven by lower workover expenses in the Gulf of America. These favorable impacts were partially offset by a $51.3 million increase in other operating activities, net, primarily related to contract prepayments in Vietnam and foreign exchange rate fluctuations. Cash Required by Investing Activities Net cash required by investing activities for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 was $510.3 million higher compared to the same period in 2025. The increase was primarily due to higher property additions and dry hole costs ($506.0 million). A reconciliation of \u201cProperty additions and dry hole costs\u201d in the Consolidated Statements of Cash Flows to total capital expenditures for continuing operations follows.\nNine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n(Millions of dollars) 2026 2025\nProperty additions and dry hole costs $ 1,333.0 $ 827.0\nAcquisition of oil and natural gas properties 28.6 24.4\nGeophysical and other exploration expenses 57.8 46.8\nCapital expenditure accrual changes and other 5.4 (30.6)\nTotal capital expenditures $ 1,424.8 $ 867.6\nTotal accrual basis capital expenditures are shown below.\nNine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n(Millions of dollars) 2026 2025\nCapital Expenditures\nExploration and production $ 1,397.6 $ 858.4\nCorporate 27.2 9.2\nTotal capital expenditures $ 1,424.8 $ 867.6\n"}
{"page": 46, "text": "Higher capital expenditures in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to the same period of 2025 were primarily attributable to higher exploratory drilling in C\u00f4te d'Ivoire, higher exploratory spending and development drilling in the Gulf of America and in Vietnam. These increases were partially offset by lower development spending in the Eagle Ford Shale and the prior year purchase of the Pioneer FPSO vessel in the Gulf of America. 40 Table of Contents ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT\u2019S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS (CONTINUED) Financial Condition (Continued) Development capital expenditures in 2026 primarily related to development drilling and field development activities in the Gulf of America ($447.8 million), the Eagle Ford Shale ($232.2 million), the Tupper Montney and the Kaybob Duvernay ($128.1 million), and in Vietnam ($99.4 million). Exploration expenditures in 2026 were $471.0 million, primarily comprised of activities in C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire related to exploration drilling for the Bubale exploration and appraisal program (Blocks CI-709 and CI-103), and the Civette-1X (Block CI-502) and Caracal-1X (Block CI-102) exploration wells. Exploration spending was also driven by activities in the Gulf of America, including federal lease acquisitions and exploration drilling at the Cello #1 and Banjo #1 exploration wells (Mississippi Canyon 385), and in Vietnam, including the Hai Su Vang (Golden Sea Lion) appraisal campaign (Blocks 15-2/17 and 15-1/05) and the Lac Da Trang (White Camel) North (Block 15-1/05) exploration well. Cash Required by Financing Activities Net cash required by financing activities for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 decreased by $111.0 million compared to the same period in 2025. In 2026, net cash required by financing activities was principally from the aggregate redemption of the 2027 Notes ($78.9 million) and 2028 Notes ($148.6 million), net repayments on the Amended RCF ($100.0 million), year-to-date cash dividends to shareholders of $1.05 per share ($150.5 million), debt issue costs for the upsize and extension of the Amended RCF and 2034 Notes bond issuance ($20.5 million), and distributions to the noncontrolling interest in MP GOM ($21.2 million). These amounts were partially offset by a refinancing transaction whereby new 2034 Notes were issued in the aggregate amount of $500.0 million. In 2025, net cash required by financing activities was principally for the repurchase of common shares ($102.6 million), year-to-date cash dividends to shareholders of $0.975 per share ($139.8 million), and distributions to the noncontrolling interest in MP GOM ($43.2 million), and was partially offset by net borrowings on the senior unsecured guaranteed RCF ($150.0 million).\n"}
{"page": 47, "text": "Liquidity At September 30, 2026 the Company had approximately $2.5 billion of liquidity consisting of $500.3 million in cash and cash equivalents and $2.0 billion available on its committed senior unsecured guaranteed Amended RCF with a major banking consortium. The Company\u2019s $2.0 billion Amended RCF expires in January 2031. As of September 30, 2026 the Company had no outstanding borrowings under the Amended RCF and $0.4 million of outstanding letters of credit, which reduce the borrowing capacity of the Amended RCF. At September 30, 2026 the interest rate in effect on borrowings under the Amended RCF was 6.16%. At September 30, 2026, the Company was in compliance with all covenants related to the Amended RCF. Cash and invested cash are maintained in several operating locations outside the U.S. As of September 30, 2026 cash and cash equivalents held outside the U.S. included U.S. dollar equivalents of approximately $105.1 million, the majority of which was held in Canada ($60.1 million), C\u00f4te d'Ivoire ($11.9 million), Vietnam ($9.5 million), Mexico ($7.7 million), Brunei ($5.4 million), and the U.K. ($4.4 million). In certain cases, the Company could incur cash taxes or other costs should these cash balances be repatriated to the U.S. in future periods. Canada currently collects a 5% withholding tax on any earnings repatriated to the U.S. Working Capital\n(Millions of dollars) September 30, 2026 December 31, 2025\nWorking capital\nTotal current assets $ 1,065.1 $ 816.7\nTotal current liabilities 1,122.3 1,062.7\nNet working capital liability $ (57.2) $ (246.0)\nAs of September 30, 2026 net working capital increased by $188.8 million compared to December 31, 2025. The increase was primarily attributable to a higher cash balance at the end of the period ($123.1 million) and higher accounts receivable ($112.5 million), partially offset by higher accounts payable ($50.6 million). 41 Table of Contents ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT\u2019S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS (CONTINUED) Financial Condition (Continued) Higher accounts receivable and accounts payable were primarily due to higher oil and NGL prices, and drilling activities in the Gulf of America, respectively. Capital Employed A summary of capital employed at September 30, 2026 and December 31, 2025 follows.\nSeptember 30, 2026 December 31, 2025\n(Millions of dollars) Amount % Amount %\nCapital employed\nLong-term debt $ 1,623.1 23.2 % $ 1,382.6 21.3 %\nMurphy shareholders' equity 5,384.9 76.8 % 5,118.4 78.7 %\nTotal capital employed $ 7,008.0 100.0 % $ 6,501.0 100.0 %\n"}
{"page": 48, "text": "At September 30, 2026, long-term debt of $1,623.1 million increased by $240.5 million compared to December 31, 2025 primarily as a result of a refinancing transaction whereby the Company issued $500.0 million of 2034 Notes and used the proceeds to redeem the 2027 Notes and 2028 Notes and pay down amounts drawn on the Amended RCF. The total of the fixed-rate notes had a weighted average maturity of 8.4 years and a weighted average coupon of 6.3%. In addition, during the quarter, the Company commenced a finance lease for a FSO supporting operations at the Lac Da Vang field in Vietnam, resulting in a $75.3 million increase in long-term debt. Murphy shareholders\u2019 equity increased by $266.5 million in 2026, primarily due to net income ($456.3 million) and ($24.1 million) stock award amortization, partially offset by dividends ($150.5 million) and foreign currency translation ($59.0 million). A summary of transactions in stockholders\u2019 equity accounts is presented in the \u201cConsolidated Statements of Stockholders\u2019 Equity\u201d on page 6 of this Form 10-Q report.\nCritical Accounting Estimates As of September 30, 2026 there have been no significant changes to our critical accounting estimates since our Annual Report on Form 10-K for the year ended December 31, 2025.\nAccounting Changes and Recent Accounting Pronouncements See Note B to the Consolidated Financial Statements regarding the impact or potential impact of recent accounting pronouncements upon our financial position and results of operations.\n42 Table of Contents ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT\u2019S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS (CONTINUED) Other Key Performance Metrics The Company uses other operational performance and income metrics to review operational performance. Management uses adjusted net income, earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization (EBITDA), adjusted EBITDA, earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and