{"page": 51, "text": "The regional conflict involving Iran continues to contribute to heightened geopolitical risk and volatility in global energy markets, including disruptions and security risks affecting transit through the Strait of Hormuz. The Company has not experienced any direct physical disruption to its operations as a result of the conflict. However, the conflict may indirectly affect the Company through fluctuations in oil and natural gas prices and demand, increased costs or reduced availability of equipment and services, and broader supply chain pressures. A prolonged or escalating conflict, including further disruptions to critical energy infrastructure or maritime routes, could increase market volatility and adversely affect economic conditions, which could affect the Company\u2019s future operating results, cash flows and financial position. Changes in tariffs and trade policies may affect the cost and availability of goods and services used in E&P operations or contribute to inflation in the countries in which the Company operates. In July 2026, the Trump Administration implemented tariffs of generally 10% or 12.5% on imports from 60 economies pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974. The Company continues to monitor the actual and potential effects of these tariffs, other trade restrictions and related governmental actions, as well as opportunities to mitigate their impacts. The ultimate effects of these measures on the Company\u2019s supply chain, capital expenditures and operating costs remain uncertain. The Company cannot predict the impact economic conditions, including inflation, trade policies, tariffs, other trade restrictions and a possible economic recession, may have on future commodity prices or the cost and availability of goods and services used in E&P operations. Similarly, the Company cannot predict the effects of political instability, armed conflict, sanctions, changes in sanctions policy or other governmental actions affecting oil- and natural-gas-producing regions, including Russia and Ukraine, the Middle East and Venezuela. Such developments may affect global oil and natural gas supply, demand and pricing. It is also uncertain how production decisions by OPEC and participating non-OPEC countries, compliance with production targets and changes in participation or production capacity may influence commodity prices and the global supply-demand balance. Lower commodity prices or higher costs, should they occur, would reduce earnings and operating cash flows and could result in material future impairment charges. For the fourth quarter of 2026, production is expected to average between 174.0 and 182.0 thousand barrels of oil equivalent per day, excluding noncontrolling interest. The Company now also expects full-year 2026 production, excluding noncontrolling interest, to be between 176.0 and 178.0 thousand barrels of oil equivalent per day, compared with its prior guidance of 167.0 to 175.0 thousand barrels of oil equivalent per day.\n"}
{"page": 52, "text": "The increased guidance is primarily attributable to accelerated Eagle Ford Shale activity, new well outperformance and lower royalties at Tupper Montney. The Company expects full-year 2026 capital expenditures, excluding noncontrolling interest, to be between $1,500 million and $1,600 million. In the Gulf of America, the Chinook #8 (Walker Ridge 425) well commenced production during the quarter. Murphy will also continue progressing the Cello #1 (Mississippi Canyon 385) and Banjo #1 (Mississippi Canyon 385) wells, which were determined to be successful in the first quarter of 2026. The Company currently expects these wells to begin production in the fourth quarter of 2027. Finally, the evaluation of multiple blocks acquired in federal lease sales is ongoing. 46 Table of Contents ITEM 2. MANAGEMENT\u2019S DISCUSSION AND ANALYSIS OF FINANCIAL CONDITION AND RESULTS OF OPERATIONS (CONTINUED) Outlook (Continued) In C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire, the Company drilled a successful appraisal well at Bubale West-1X (Block CI-103). Going forward, the Bubale appraisal program is expected to include two to four wells over the next 18 to 24 months. The Company commenced production at Lac Da Vang (Golden Camel), Block 15-1/05 in Vietnam during the quarter, and also drilled a discovery at the Lac Da Trang (White Camel) North-1X (Block 15-1/05) exploration well. At Hai Su Vang (Golden Sea Lion), the Company is evaluating development alternatives and advancing post-appraisal studies, with a final investment decision currently anticipated in late 2027. In addition to ongoing activities in C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire and Vietnam, the Company continues to pursue future exploration opportunities internationally. Subsequent to the third quarter, the Company signed agreements with the government of Mauritania for two offshore exploration blocks, and is working to finalize an agreement in Cameroon before year-end. Capital and other expenditures are routinely reviewed and planned capital expenditures may be adjusted to reflect differences between budgeted and forecast cash flow during the year. Capital expenditures may also be affected by asset purchases or sales, as well as changing commodity price environments, which often are not anticipated at the time a budget is prepared. The Company will primarily fund its capital program in 2026 using operating cash flow and available cash. If oil and/or natural gas prices weaken, actual cash flow generated from operations could be reduced such that capital spending reductions are required and/or borrowings under available credit facilities might be required during the year to maintain funding of the Company\u2019s ongoing development projects. The Company plans to utilize any surplus cash (not planned to be used by operations, investing activities, dividends or payment to noncontrolling interests) in accordance with the Company\u2019s capital allocation plan designed to allow for additional shareholder returns and debt reduction. Details of the plan can be found in the\n"}
{"page": 53, "text": "\u201cCapital Allocation\u201d section of the Company\u2019s Form 8-K filed on May 7, 2025. Based on current market conditions, our planned exploration and appraisal program and our bond terms, the Company is currently more likely to use available adjusted free cash flow for share repurchases than bond repayment. On August 8, 2024, the Company\u2019s Board of Directors authorized a share repurchase program whereby the Company can repurchase up to $1,100 million of the Company\u2019s common stock, of which $550 million remains available to repurchase as of September 30, 2026. The Company continues to monitor the impact of commodity prices on its financial position and is currently in compliance with the covenants related to the senior unsecured guaranteed Amended RCF (see Note E). As of November 2, 2026 the Company has entered into forward fixed price delivery contracts to manage risk associated with certain future oil and natural gas sales prices as follows:\nVolumes\n(MMCF/d) Price/MCF Remaining Period\nArea Commodity Type 1 Start Date End Date\nCanada Natural Gas Fixed price forward sales 59 C$3.00 10/1/2026 12/31/2026\nCanada Natural Gas Fixed price forward sales 9.5 C$3.14 1/1/2027 12/31/2027\n1 Fixed price forward sale contracts listed above are accounted for as normal sales and purchases for accounting purposes.\n"}
